     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hello all,<div><br></div><div>For the sake of full disclosure, I would like to point out that I have already posted this question on <a href=\"http://stackoverflow.com/questions/24152177/controlling-spacing-in-custom-ggplot2-geoms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://stackoverflow.com/questions/24152177/controlling-spacing-in-custom-ggplot2-geoms&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792370687120000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3FWpvCWOELWV1PnVBMyDTY\">stack overflow</a>, so please feel free to answer here, there, both... whatever!</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>I am creating a custom ggplot geom to represent Edward Tufte&#39;s 2nd boxplot:</div><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"><a href=\"http://i.stack.imgur.com/LsZIL.png\" style=\"margin-left:1em;margin-right:1em\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://i.stack.imgur.com/LsZIL.png&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792370687120000&amp;usg=AOvVaw04LPwCe37-V4nMKYg2Gc6Z\"><img src=\"https://ci4.googleusercontent.com/proxy/Gl0enBSP0G-8LT1D7jb-sR-UDhoo5w441Rm5RyIUPt_oKMWugEMOcWdyLlZ9gCQYcD4hFXM=s0-d-e1-ft#http://i.stack.imgur.com/LsZIL.png\" border=\"0\" width=\"320\" height=\"209\"></a></p><div><div>An eventual goal is to be able to use this as an axis to show marginal distributions succinctly.</div><div>I am basing my work on <a href=\"http://www.jrnold.me/about.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.jrnold.me/about.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792370687120000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3Y81brSG50lXseTKMOaH2E\">Jeffrey Arnold</a>&#39;s <a href=\"https://github.com/jrnold/ggthemes\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/jrnold/ggthemes&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792370687120000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0CvWYXpkNv9i4FCpSGWpHE\">ggthemes</a>.</div></div><div><br></div><div>I have gotten a output that looks right, but making it work in a general case has led to the question at hand.<br></div><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"><a href=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-DMIN-GegS8o/U5g9cV7ZVOI/AAAAAAAAAmQ/g7Z9_PUCBnI/s1600/tufte_boxplot.png\" style=\"margin-left:1em;margin-right:1em\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-DMIN-GegS8o/U5g9cV7ZVOI/AAAAAAAAAmQ/g7Z9_PUCBnI/s1600/tufte_boxplot.png&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792370687120000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3Y30fCxjj1GJif4e0hbZtG\"><img src=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-DMIN-GegS8o/U5g9cV7ZVOI/AAAAAAAAAmQ/g7Z9_PUCBnI/s320/tufte_boxplot.png\" border=\"0\" width=\"320\" height=\"207\"></a></p><div>Below is the code that is used to draw the middle lines (i.e. the lines that replace the box):<br></div><div><br></div><div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">    median.thickness &lt;- 0.25</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">    box.offset &lt;- 0.01</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">    boxdata &lt;- data.frame(</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">        x = data$x + box.offset,</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">        xend = data$x + box.offset,</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">        y = c(data$upper, data$lower),</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">        yend = c(data$middle + median.thickness, data$middle - median.thickness),</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">        alpha = NA,</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">        common)</font></div></div><div><br></div><div><div><br></div><div>Clearly the values <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">median.thickness</font> and <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">box.offset</font> will need to be different depending range of the plot, so it would be ideal to compute them based on those values. Is there a way to accomplish this in a geom?</div></div><div><br></div></div>"
